WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.133
Telemetry becomes much more useful when signals share consistent context.

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:07.359
A metric that says latency increased is helpful.

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:11.795
A metric that also includes the service, environment, cluster, namespace, workload,

00:00:11.795 --> 00:00:16.635
model version, deployment version, tenant class, and route is far more actionable.

00:00:16.635 --> 00:00:20.668
Labels and identifiers turn isolated signals into an investigation path.

00:00:20.668 --> 00:00:25.104
Important context may include service name, environment, cluster, region, namespace, node,

00:00:25.104 --> 00:00:29.943
workload, deployment version, model name, model version, request class, data source, tenant,

00:00:29.943 --> 00:00:32.766
request ID, trace ID, and correlation ID.

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:35.186
Not every signal needs every label.

00:00:35.186 --> 00:00:40.025
The design decision is to include the labels that help teams answer

00:00:40.025 --> 00:00:42.445
likely questions without creating uncontrolled cost,

00:00:42.445 --> 00:00:44.058
privacy, or cardinality problems.

00:00:44.058 --> 00:00:49.704
Correlation helps teams connect a slow user request to a specific service, model deployment,

00:00:49.704 --> 00:00:52.527
GPU node, retrieval index, or downstream dependency.

00:00:52.527 --> 00:00:57.367
When a request ID appears in logs, traces, events, and selected metrics,

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.400
teams can move from symptom to cause with less guesswork.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:07.449
This is especially important in hybrid systems where ownership may cross teams and infrastructure boundaries.

00:01:07.449 --> 00:01:09.869
Too little metadata makes investigation slow.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:13.902
Too much ungoverned metadata creates noise, cost, and privacy risk.

00:01:13.902 --> 00:01:15.515
Naming conventions, label discipline,

00:01:15.515 --> 00:01:19.951
and correlation rules should be treated as architecture decisions, not afterthoughts.

00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:21.967
Good context makes telemetry explainable.

00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:28.420
It gives responders enough shared language to ask better questions during routine operations and during incidents.
